> with ID, naming, #adjust, or SOMETHING, I'm just unable to find the
> exact mechanism... is there one?

1. Drop all dagger you *don't* was to stack together.
2. Name the remaining daggers to a space. That is type the following
character in quotes " " Then press return. This removes any name they
have. Do this as well to uniquely name them to nothing. 

3. Naming only does two things: it uniquely identifies a *group* of one
item you think are cursed for example. It names (for example a scroll) an
object of a certain type. You can nun-uniquely name a scroll "remove
curse" and any further scroll with the same markings will be called that.
You can then name one scroll named "remove curse" that doesn't stack with
the others "cursed". That's all it does. You *can't* separate one item out
of a stack without never picking another item like it again. You *will*
eventually find another item that stacks with it. 

4. For daggers what I do, is I *don't* name or identify them until I get
20 that stack. These you can pretty well guarantee will be +0 uncursed.
the number is high enough that getting cursed or some other enchantment is
*very* remote. 

5. When you buy something in a store - other than scrolls - as soon as you
leave the store, immediately drop all items of that type and then pick
them up again. slash'em has *big* problems with stacking. Nethack only has
it for unindentified food under certain circumstances, and sometimes with
stores - although that may just be spork. In slash'em stacking pretty well
messes up everywhere. Dropping and picking up items of one type until
you're sure they're properly stacked is something to try to remember in
slash'em. It's annoying, but you pretty well have to do it. 

6. Reserve naming for grey stones, scrolls, wands, rings, special
armour/weapons - speed boots, potions and misc items like bags of holding
vs. sacks. These all by class as opposed to uniquely.



